WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.580
Mark Hamill: It’s hard to promote [the movie
BRIGSBY BEAR] without being able to be specific…

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:05.000
Film Courage: Yeah…I know.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.620
Mark Hamill: It’s my fondest hope that people
will go because the best way to see it is

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:13.889
completely cold without any preconceived notions.

00:00:13.889 --> 00:00:19.350
And I got to see [this movie] in Sundance
with an audience which is always exciting.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:26.900
Because if you go to a studio screening everybody’s
predisposed to really like it.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:31.369
Here was an audience that was really honest
and then the reaction was wonderful.

00:00:31.369 --> 00:00:33.300
Film Courage: Well I’m wondering if we can
role play for a moment actually?

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:34.300
Kyle Mooney: Oh…wow?

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.360
Film Courage: Just a quick role play.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:37.360
Kyle Mooney: Okay?

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:38.360
Yeah…I’m listening…

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:39.890
Film Courage: It will be PG.

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:41.500
It will be totally PG-13.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:42.500
Kyle Mooney: Okay.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:45.610
Film Courage: We won’t offend anyone or
anything like that.

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:49.700
Can you practice or show us sort of how you
pitched Mark Hamill the script [for BRIGSBY

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:50.753
BEAR] without giving away the incredible premise.

00:00:50.753 --> 00:00:51.840
I know this is the tough part.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.510
Mark Hamill: You didn’t have to!

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:59.030
Kyle Mooney: The Hollywood thing is there
are people who are doing all of these things

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:00.030
kind of for you.

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:06.110
Like we wrote the script and then our producers
(I imagine) got it in the hands of either

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:08.530
your daughter or your representation?

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:10.550
Mark Hamill: Probably my daughter.

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:13.280
Kyle Mooney: And then you had a chance to
read it.Mark Hamill: She runs everything.

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:14.710
I don’t know what’s going on.

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:18.440
Kyle Mooney: We were fortunate in that one
of our producers is friends with Chelsea so…

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:19.660
Mark Hamill: Is that right?

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:20.660
Kyle Mooney: Right?

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:21.660
That is correct.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:23.030
She’s over here [points off camera].

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:24.030
Film Courage: Oh!

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.030
Great choice.

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:26.030
Awesome choice!

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:27.030
Kyle Mooney: But we can still do the role
play!

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:28.030
Film Courage: Okay!

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:29.030
Let’s do it.

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:35.430
Kyle Mooney: Here…here’s what it would
have been like “Hey, Bill over at Lonely

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:41.680
Island, can you get this…or Will over at
Lord and Miller, can you get this script over

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:45.080
to Chelsea Hamel to potentially get it to
Mark Hamill?”

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:46.540
“We will do our best!”

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:47.540
“Okay, thank you!”

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:51.010
And I imagine you get a phone call or an email…

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:52.080
Mark Hamill: “Send me a deal memo!

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:53.640
I don’t care about the script.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.940
I just want to know how much I can make!

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:59.640
Do I get any back-end?”

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:05.310
Believe me, at this point in my career, the
script is everything.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:08.670
I was saying to Kyle earlier, I don’t even
know how much I made for this movie because

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:14.330
once I like something and you factor in that
they don’t have a huge budget.

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:16.130
That’s what I love about this.

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:19.520
George [Lucas] called STAR WARS the most expensive
low-budget movie ever made.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:21.550
Kyle Mooney: And which George?

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:28.430
Mark Hamill: Oh, he’s retired now but his
point was that all the money is up on the

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:29.430
screen.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:32.830
The only place they had to pay real money
to were Sir Alec Guinness and Peter Cushing

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:35.680
because we were all pretty much unknowns.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:41.650
And with this I was set up in the wrong way
because it’s called BRIGSBY BEAR and it’s

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:44.230
co-written by Kyle Mooney [and Kevin Costello]
and it’s directed by the Saturday Night

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:48.460
Live guy [Dave McCary] and Andy Samberg is
connected and Beck Bennett and Michaela Watkins,

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:49.740
so I was expecting these people.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:57.959
So I was expecting this broad, over-the-top
comedy and it was so unexpected.

00:02:57.959 --> 00:03:01.300
I loved it from the first time I read it.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:04.750
Film Courage: Did the character of Ted take
any convincing [for you to want to portray]?

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:07.770
Because I know he is a complicated person
(again without trying to give away too much

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:09.020
information).

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:12.190
But I think I had read something in the press
notes Mark that you said that he’s the kind

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:15.099
of character that you’d rather watch than
play.

00:03:15.099 --> 00:03:18.420
I mean there is a lot of layers to him.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:19.420
Mark Hamill: Yeah.

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:20.420
He’s dark.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:23.430
Well again, it wasn't from the standpoint
that I have to play a character that everyone

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:24.959
admires.

00:03:24.959 --> 00:03:30.640
You know, I love playing villainous roles
but no villain ever thinks of themselves as

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:31.640
evil.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:34.620
They did the right thing in their estimation.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:40.730
But there is no getting around he did something
that terrifies any parent.

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:43.300
That’s like their worst fear.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:49.280
But again, that wasn’t so important in the
larger scheme of things.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:52.940
I was just trying to understand a person that
would do that.

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:58.850
And I think Jane Adams who plays my wife does
a lot in making you understand their relationship

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:02.190
and how controlling and aggressive she is.

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:08.340
But she brings a lot more to the table than
the script so we were really lucky to have

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:09.340
her.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:13.620
Film Courage: Kyle are you aware of how other
people who are fan obsessed are going to relate

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:18.949
to this film because it shows such a great…I
mean it’s an original story but it’s also

00:04:18.949 --> 00:04:23.339
sort of a wonderful tribute to you and all
of your Good Neighbor (Youtube Sketch Comedy

00:04:23.339 --> 00:04:26.080
Group) on being obsessed and making something.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:28.439
Wondering if you can talk about that?

00:04:28.439 --> 00:04:34.050
Kyle Mooney: Well…I’m an obsessive person
and I’ve had so many obsessions throughout

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:35.460
my life.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:39.520
Star Wars being one of them at one point.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:45.430
And yeah it’s true there are people that
love our videos and followed us for so long

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:53.129
and I hope they come along to see this because…it’s
not necessarily what those videos are, it’s

00:04:53.129 --> 00:04:54.129
different.

00:04:54.129 --> 00:05:03.250
But in terms of obsession and fandom, what
you see on screen is the way a lot of us feel.

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:09.430
We have such a strong connection to these
TV shows or movies or music and it’s not

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:11.110
always a terrible thing.

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:16.680
In the case of this movie, [Kyle’s character
protaganist James Pope] love for Brigsby Bear

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:22.080
kind of unites him and his friends and his
family and they make this thing together.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.229
Mark Hamill: You never outgrew….you out
grow these things, right?

00:05:26.229 --> 00:05:31.030
You said “There was a point where I liked
STAR WARS” because basically our fans are

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:32.199
the young and the mentally young.

00:05:32.199 --> 00:05:33.199
Kyle Mooney: No.

00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:34.659
My point is I’ve never outgrown STAR WARS.

00:05:34.659 --> 00:05:39.469
My point was there was a specific time where
I was probably the most obsessed about it.

00:05:39.469 --> 00:05:41.150
Now it’s turned into a minor obsession.

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:42.909
Mark Hamill: [Laughs] Okay.

00:05:42.909 --> 00:05:43.909
Just checking.

00:05:43.909 --> 00:05:44.909
It’s okay.

00:05:44.909 --> 00:05:48.080
I mean people come up and say “I’m really
sorry I’ve never seen STAR WARS.”

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:49.330
Well, that’s fine!

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:50.879
I’m not upset.

00:05:50.879 --> 00:05:51.879
That’s okay.

00:05:51.879 --> 00:05:55.720
It’s not for everybody, you know.

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:57.470
Like I said, the young and the mentally young.

00:05:57.470 --> 00:06:01.159
Those are the ones that are really our biggest
fans.

00:06:01.159 --> 00:06:08.529
There is something to be said about it appeals
to the child in all of us because you see

00:06:08.529 --> 00:06:16.039
grown men, policemen and firemen who are authority
figures let down their guard and become 9-years-old

00:06:16.039 --> 00:06:17.039
again.

00:06:17.039 --> 00:06:19.389
Like “Will you pose with this lightsaber?”

00:06:19.389 --> 00:06:20.389
“Really!?

00:06:20.389 --> 00:06:22.529
You were just about to arrest me.

00:06:22.529 --> 00:06:24.029
Now you’re my friend?”

00:06:24.029 --> 00:06:31.219
It’s been amazing because I never expected
it to have this longevity or the impact on

00:06:31.219 --> 00:06:32.219
these people.

00:06:32.219 --> 00:06:35.789
And you meet people all the time that say
“I became what I became because of that

00:06:35.789 --> 00:06:36.789
movie.”

00:06:36.789 --> 00:06:44.349
So it’s quite…it’s awesome when you
think of the impact that it’s had after

00:06:44.349 --> 00:06:45.349
all these years.

00:06:45.349 --> 00:06:47.779
I certainly never thought I’d still be talking
about it.

00:06:47.779 --> 00:06:50.340
Kyle Mooney: And that’s what we’re trying
to do with BRIGSBY BEAR.

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:55.870
Mark Hamill: And I think you’re well on
your way because it is like a valentine to

00:06:55.870 --> 00:07:01.919
obsessed compulsives, you know?

00:07:01.919 --> 00:07:05.430
You’re saying it’s okay to be this way.

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:10.740
I love the fact that James is so accepted
by the teenagers you’ll think ‘Well gosh

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:15.860
someone that innocent, you know he is almost
like Rip Van Winkle where he wakes up in a

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:18.620
world he never made.

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:24.469
I love the scene with the teenage party they’re
having and he doesn’t know all of the vernacular

00:07:24.469 --> 00:07:26.990
(he’s using some of the slang wrong).

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:32.770
And instead of being cynical and ostracizing
and making fun of him, he’s accepted.

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:35.330
I mean it’s charming, it really is.

